        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Modernism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, above all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>; Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin-america">Latin America</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uruguay">Uruguay</a>); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shanghai">Shanghai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>; anglophone <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">precursors from the mid-19th century (Baudelaire and Manet; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/impressionism">Impressionism</a> and symbolism from the 1860s); the first wave, 1901–1930 (Fauvism, Cubism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expressionism">Expressionism</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/futurism">Futurism</a>, atonality, stream of consciousness, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dada">Dada</a>, Surrealism); continuing 1930–1945 under fascism and socialist realism; late modernism after 1945 (Abstract Expressionism, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> of the Absurd, minimalism), with postmodernism established in art and architecture by the early 1980s</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val">Charles Baudelaire and Manet, early modernists for Clement Greenberg; the painters <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso">Pablo Picasso</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-matisse">Henri Matisse</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky">Wassily Kandinsky</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Arnold Schoenberg</a>, composer of atonal and twelve-tone music; Igor Stravinsky, composer of The Rite of Spring; the novelists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-joyce">James Joyce</a>, Virginia Woolf and Marcel Proust; the poets T. S. Eliot and Ezra Pound; the architects Frank Lloyd Wright and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-corbusier">Le Corbusier</a>; Marcel Duchamp; Jackson Pollock</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">by 1930 it had won a place in the political and artistic establishment; banned as “degenerate” by the Nazis and ousted by socialist realism in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; after 1945 it continued as late modernism, and was followed or extended, depending on the critic, by postmodernism; museums of modern art in most major cities</span></div>
        </div>
