    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of Modernism</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-darwin.jpg" alt="Charles Darwin" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Charles Darwin <span class="kid-tale-cr">Henry Maull (1829–1914) and John Fox (1832–1907) (Maull &amp; Fox) [3]; derivative work by Beao, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 150 years ago, new ideas, like those of Charles Darwin, made many people doubt old beliefs.</p><p>Modernists were artists and writers who turned away from old traditions to find new ways of making art.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pablo-picasso.jpg" alt="Pablo Picasso" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Pablo Picasso <span class="kid-tale-cr">Argentina. Revista Vea y Lea, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 100 years ago, Pablo Picasso and other painters began a new style called Cubism.</p><p>Painters stopped copying nature and made colour and shape the heart of their art.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-joyce.jpg" alt="James Joyce" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>James Joyce <span class="kid-tale-cr">Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Writers like James Joyce and Virginia Woolf wrote stories that follow the flow of a character's thoughts.</p><p>Many people were shocked, and some of the new books, poems and music were hard to understand.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/expressionism.jpg" alt="Expressionism" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Expressionism <span class="kid-tale-cr">Rudolf Ledl / Fritz Bernhard, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The Nazis insulted modern art as &quot;degenerate&quot; and stopped many artists from showing or even making their art.</p><p>Many artists left for the Americas, and after the war New York became the new centre of art.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wassily-kandinsky.jpg" alt="Wassily Kandinsky" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Wassily Kandinsky <span class="kid-tale-cr">Vassily Kandinsky by Adolf Elnain Photo credits : Georges Meguerditchian - Centre Pompidou, MNAM-CCI /Dist. RMN-GP Im..., Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Wassily Kandinsky made art from pure colour, and called one of his works the first abstract painting.</p><p>Today most big cities have museums of modern art, such as the Tate Modern in London.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Modernism grew out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>’s revolt against industrial and bourgeois society and was shaped by the ideas of Darwin, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karl-marx">Marx</a>, Nietzsche, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Freud</a> and Bergson.</li><li>In the first decade of the 20th century Cubism, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/expressionism">Expressionism</a>, abstract painting and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arnold-schoenberg">Schoenberg</a>’s atonal music broke with the public’s idea of art, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the modernist view of reality became more generally accepted.</li><li>Condemned as “degenerate” by the Nazis and ousted by socialist realism in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, it found a new centre in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> after 1945; when it ended, if it did, is still contested.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>In 1917 Marcel Duchamp submitted a urinal, signed “R. Mutt” and named Fountain, as a sculpture for the inaugural exhibition of the Society of Independent Artists in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, asking people to see it as a work of art because he said it was one.</p></aside>
    </section>

